-- **Weapons vs abilities.** Anything fired with an attack button is a weapon. A shot fired from its own ability button (a grenade, a rocket volley on a cooldown) is an ability, not a weapon. Abilities are counted by the rule under `abilities[]` below. A passive or triggered effect is its own ability only when that rule asks for one: Paired entries asks for it, or the source names the effect and it adds a mechanic. An effect that is part of how a weapon works (an infester hidden inside its host, or escaping when the host dies) stays in that weapon's summary and patterns. A swap is an ability only when the source lists it as an ability or command with its own name (Electro-Stasis Ray, Leech Beam, special-ammo commands). Switching a seat's weapons with number keys or a scroll wheel, as in shooters, is not: each weapon is its own primary, secondary or option entry. A weapon may link a pattern whose generator lists only ability kinds (the drone's claws link `infest-vehicle`). The generator makes that an ability on a card, so do not add one to the source unit. Unarmed units have an empty weapon list.
-- **Weapon entries.** Count weapon entries by these rules. (1) One in-game weapon is one entry, even when it acts differently against different targets. Code its family and traits as it works against its main target: the target its primary role is about, or else the one the source names first. Its damage and delivery follow their own rules (`armament[].damage` step 5 when its damage category differs by target; the order under `armament[].delivery`). List every target it can hit, and put the other behaviour in the summary. (2) Among the fixed weapons of one seat, make one entry per family. Copies of one gun are one entry (twin cannons, a squad's five rifles). So are different guns of one family that the seat uses on the same attack order, at once or each against its own kind of target (a stock sergeant's carbine with the squad's rifles, a ground battery with an air battery). Name them all and list every target any of them can hit. A gun of another family is its own entry. A gun of the same family on its own key or button (a pistol beside a rifle) is its own entry too. Where players set their own fire groups (MechWarrior), each family on a seat is one entry. (3) Rule 2 never merges a loadout choice, or a weapon the unit does not always have. Alternate, passenger, acquired and option weapons are counted by their own rules. (4) Versions of one weapon across the titles of a series entry (the same place in the loadout, renamed or retuned) are one entry. Code the version the Series games convention picks, even when an older version works differently, and put the differences in the summary. (5) Loadout choices for one place are one entry when they would get the same family, damage, delivery, target list and traits. Name them all; range and other numbers may differ. Otherwise each choice is its own entry (Smart Rockets home in, so they are apart). (6) A weapon whose kind depends on the donor is one adaptive acquired entry. Do not list the donors. Weapons gained in fixed steps (salvage crates) get one acquired entry per step that changes the family. (7) Passenger weapons follow the passenger slot rule. Riders who fire their own guns from ports give no entries. (8) Never record an upgrade that only changes numbers, or a weapon the unit gets through a shared mechanic or a universal key (see Shared mechanics). Protection that only shoots down incoming missiles, rockets or shells by itself is not a weapon either: it is the interceptor armour model.
