t the same family, damage, delivery, target list and traits. Name them all; range and other numbers may differ. Otherwise each choice is its own entry (Smart Rockets home in, so they are apart). (6) A weapon whose kind depends on the donor is one adaptive acquired entry. Do not list the donors. Weapons gained in fixed steps (salvage crates) get one acquired entry per step that changes the family. (7) Passenger weapons follow the passenger slot rule. Riders who fire their own guns from ports give no entries. (8) Never record an upgrade that only changes numbers, or a weapon the unit gets through a shared mechanic or a universal key (see Shared mechanics). Protection that only shoots down incoming missiles, rockets or shells by itself is not a weapon either: it is the interceptor armour model.
 - **Main weapon.** A unit's main weapon is its one primary entry (see `armament[].slot`). Every other weapon the base form always has is secondary, including a second seat's main gun. The roles rules read the main weapon from this entry, so it is chosen without looking at the unit's roles.
 - **Paired entries.** Some weapons always come with an ability, and both entries of the pair link the same patterns. A weapon swapped in by an ability is an alternate weapon plus a toggle ability (deploy when the unit cannot move in one of the two states). Both link one swap pattern: the mode's own pattern (its `modes[].via`, such as `deploy-siege-mode`) when the swap comes with a mode, otherwise `switch-weapon-mode`. Both also link the pattern of what the swapped-in weapon does, if one fits. The weapon that is swapped out links no swap pattern. A weapon gained from a kill or a wreck is an acquired weapon plus a triggered ability, both linking the gain pattern (`leech-weapon-on-kill`). A weapon added by a bought upgrade or research has no ability; it links `upgrade-in-field` when the upgrade reaches units already in the field (bought on the unit, or for all of them at a building), and no pattern when only units built later get it. An ability that a bought upgrade or research adds links `upgrade-in-field` on the same terms as such a weapon (bought on the unit, or researched for all of them at a building, and reaching units already in the field), next to the patterns of what it does. A weapon that comes with a rank links `earn-veterancy`. A unit with passenger seats gets one passive ability for them, source innate, or source upgrade when only a bought upgrade adds the seats. It links `passenger-sets-weapon` when riders set its weapon or fire their own from it (passenger-module or passenger-firing seats), and `carry-passengers` otherwise (passenger-cargo seats), never both; each passenger weapon links `passenger-sets-weapon` too. The weapon used when nobody rides inside is a plain primary and links no passenger pattern. Load and unload buttons stay off (universal keys).
 - **Shared mechanics and universal keys.** Some mechanics belong to the game, not the unit: every unit of its class has them, on its side or in the whole game, apart from a few named exceptions. Examples are basic orders (move, attack, stop, hold, patrol, stances), Company of Heroes infantry that retreat, reinforce, garrison, capture points, pick up dropped weapons and re-crew abandoned guns, and veterancy that every unit earns. Leave them off the unit's weapons and abilities. Each game's list is written once, before any unit of that game is coded, in that batch's registry note (`research/registry-notes/games-r<batch>.md`, section Game-wide mechanics). It names each mechanic, the units that lack it, and the pattern it would link or the pattern wanted. Coders follow the list. A coder who meets a mechanic that may be game-wide but is not listed codes it on the unit and adds a fit_note, and the merge pass decides. If this unit does a listed mechanic better or worse than the rest (it captures faster), say so in its strengths or weaknesses. Leave off actions that match one of our universal keys (enter or leave, pick up, shake off, bail out, switch seat, ping, evict passengers), even when the source gives the unit its own button for it, such as Evacuate. But record what only some unit types gain from such an action (vehicles that turn salvage crates into weapons). Also record a button that does more than the key (troops that rappel onto a building and clear it). A side-wide power (a protocol, doctrine or general's power) that changes every unit of a class is left off too. A power that gives named unit types a new button or a new effect is recorded on each of them, with source commander-power.
 - **Pattern links.** Link a pattern when the ability or weapon gives the player the same interaction and the enemy the same counterplay as the pattern's summary. Fiction, delivery and size don't matter (see Granularity). Link a second pattern only when it passes the same test on its own: an ECM that hides you from radar and breaks locks links `jam-sensors` and `fire-countermeasures`. Hiding from radar or minimaps, and blocking new locks while hidden, is `jam-sensors`; breaking a lock already made or pulling missiles in flight off you is `fire-countermeasures`. Flares or chaff that also keep new locks off for a moment after firing link `fire-countermeasures` alone. Smoke links `lay-smoke` only when the sources say it blocks sight or aim. Do not link a pattern for a part that only looks like it. `capture-structure` needs an enemy building, so taking over a neutral one is not it. A thrown sticky bomb is `throw-explosive`, not `infest-vehicle`, because nobody latches on. Ending your own burning or disabled state on a key links `heal-self`, unless the patterns group adds a pattern for it. A retreat-to-base order is a game-wide mechanic in most games (see Shared mechanics); where a unit does record one, it links `retreat-to-base`, never `dash` or `brace`. When `research/registry-notes/patterns.md` disagrees with a pattern's summary, the summary wins. If nothing fits, keep the ability, leave its patterns empty and add a fit_note (see `abilities[].patterns`). Link any registered id, written or planned. Never edit an existing pattern file: the merge pass keeps its source_units up to date with `npm run sync-patterns`.
 - **Granularity.** Same player-facing interaction and same counterplay = one pattern; differences in fiction, delivery or size are variants. Plain mechanics (sprint, healing yourself, stat buff) get generic patterns that stay out of the top-30 list. A pattern that mirrors a word-list term (a weapon trait or family, an armour model, a sensor trait) is the home for the ability version only; a weapon or always-on effect uses the term. A swapped-in weapon is still a weapon, so neither it nor its toggle links a pattern that mirrors its family or trait (a drain beam on a toggle links no `drain-health`). The mirroring patterns are `mark-target`, `suppress-with-fire`, `leave-hazard-zone`, `drain-health`, `launch-drones`, `reveal-area` and `heal-self`.
-- **Bands with numbers.** Ratio bands (speed, sight, range) compare a unit with its own game's reference unit, named in the games registry. A ratio exactly on an edge goes to the band nearer medium. Threshold bands (price, cooldown) give each band's lowest value, so a value exactly on an edge takes the higher band. Divide the figures as the sources give them and round only the result. Only `cost_band.raw` and `mobility.speed_raw` hold stat figures. Every text field of a unit (basis, notes, summaries, uncertain notes and source notes) gives prices, speeds, reaches, sight, cooldowns, damage and armour values as comparisons in words ("about twice the rifle's reach", "takes a few seconds", "a little more from behind"), never as figures. Names, patch numbers, dates and counts (seats, missiles) are fine.
+- **Bands with numbers.** Ratio bands (speed, sight, range) compare a unit with its own game's reference unit, named in the games registry. A ratio exactly on an edge goes to the band nearer medium. Threshold bands (price, cooldown) give each band's lowest value, so a value exactly on an edge takes the higher band. Divide the figures as the sources give them and round only the result. Only `cost_band.raw` and `mobility.speed_raw` hold stat figures. Every text field of a unit (basis, notes, summaries, uncertain notes and source notes) gives prices, speeds, reaches, sight, cooldowns, effect durations, damage and armour values as comparisons in words ("about twice the rifle's reach", "takes a few seconds", "a little more from behind"), never as figures. A zero has nothing to compare with, so write it as none ("sees nothing in flight"). Names, patch numbers, dates and counts (seats, missiles) are fine.
 - **Stock values.** Every band describes the stock unit at the version researched: no researched or bought upgrades, no veterancy, no temporary buffs, in its default mode. Put what upgrades change in notes, and what another mode changes in modes. An ability or weapon that an upgrade unlocks is still recorded (ability source upgrade, weapon slot acquired); only its bands are taken as unlocked, before further upgrades. Passenger seats that only an upgrade adds are recorded too: put them in crew.passengers and name the upgrade in crew.notes. The body plan describes the stock unit; an add-on turret goes in body.notes. A loadout pick is not an upgrade: each field's own rule says how picks are recorded.
 - **Series games.** For a games.json entry of kind series, the counted titles are the lead titles named in `version_researched` and any title named there for the archetype being coded. Only counted titles fill fields; facts from the series' other titles go in notes. When counted titles differ, on whether a mechanic exists or on a value, the newest counted title whose sources speak to it decides, at its final patch; give the other version in notes or in the weapon's summary. A figure that the source says a later patch of that title changed, without giving the new value, does not count for that title. Where the registry entry says where a kind of fact comes from (MechWarrior speeds from the BattleTechWiki), follow the registry. When one archetype mirrors several models, one per side, take figures from the model of the first faction in the registry's list that is not shared, and say which in notes. A matchup may rest on a source about any title, as long as the parts it names exist in a counted title.
 - **Copyright.** Paraphrase only, no quoted voice lines or flavour text, no images. Real names are reference only and never reach a generated card.
